in this lesson we're going to talk about  piecewise functions so on the act at  least in the past acts when they've  presented problems like this it's  usually a multiple choice you know  problem well that's of course it's  multiple choice but what they'll do is  they'll give you a bunch of different  graphs right and you just have to select  you know which one is the appropriate  graph well what you can do on a problem  like that is of course you can pick some  test values and see which ones are going  to you know correspond to the graphs and  which ones don't but i just want to have  you do some practice on this particular  question so you can be familiar with how  these piecewise functions work to help  narrow down your choices a little bit  quicker so go ahead and pause the video  see if you can graph this function and  we'll do it together  so g of x when you see g of x or f of x  or h of x you can think of that as y so  what we have here is we have y equals x  plus 2  only when x is less than negative 2. so  less than means to the left right so  what you could do is you could graph  okay y equals x plus 2 it has a y  intercept of 2 and it has a slope of  positive one which means you're going up  one over one like this so it's a line  like this  but it's when x is less than two so an  easier way to do this is to make a  little table  and just pick values that are negative  two and smaller right we know it's going  to be a straight line because it's in  the form y equals mx plus b let's just  pick some values so negative 2 times 1  is negative 2 plus 2 is 0. negative 3  plus 2 is  negative 1 negative 4 is negative 2  negative 3. so if we plot these points  we get negative 2 0 now the important  thing here is that when x is less than  negative 2 right not including negative  2 so that means we're going to have this  as an open circle it's not going to be  closed it would only be filled in or  closed if it was equal to and then we've  got negative 3  negative 1 and negative four negative  two and you can see it's a line that's  going off in that direction to the left  so less than or to the left of negative  two  now the next one it's y equals four and  we know line the line y equals four is a  horizontal line okay right here at four  in between negative 2 and 2 including  negative 2 and 2. see the equal signs so  i'm going to go up to here i'm going to  make this a filled in circle i'm going  to go up to positive 2 that's a filled  in circle and we've got our horizontal  line the last one you can see it's an x  squared which means we know it's a  parabola the negative x squared means  it's opening down like this  and the plus 3 means we're shifting the  graph up 3. but again i'm going to use  this table method i think this is an  easy way to do it and just pick values  that are greater than 2. now i'm putting  2 here because we're interested in to  the points up to 2 but not including 2  so i'm just going to use 2 3 and 4. so 2  squared is 4 times negative 1 is  negative 4 plus 3 is negative 1. 3  squared is 9 times negative 1 is  negative 9 plus 3 is negative 6 and 4  squared is 16 times negative 1 is  negative 16 plus 3 is negative 13 right  so if we plot these  2 negative 1 this is going to be open  because again it's greater than 2 but  not equal to 2.  3 is going to be negative 6 so that's  going to be let's see 2 3 4 5 6 right  about there and then 4 is going to be  way down here at negative 13. so we can  see this graph is  it looks like a straight line but it  actually has a little bit of a curve to  it if you were to graph the entire  parabola it looks something like this  this is a little bit of a curve to it  and that's your piecewise function so  again you can pick points like i did  here to narrow down your choices when  you're doing that multiple choice  piecewise graph a question  i'll see you in the next lesson
 

